V=['vinogradov1972logic','vinogradov2015logic'];G=['grothendieck1967egaiv4']
items(241,[
I('P000377','method','Repeated commutators recover the Hamilton–Jacobi symbol',r'The 1 November entry calls \(\delta_f\cdots\delta_f\mathcal D\), with the same function everywhere, the left side of a Hamilton--Jacobi equation, and asks the audience to calculate it.',r'For a scalar differential operator \(A=\sum_{|\alpha|\le m}a_\alpha(x)\partial^\alpha\) over a smooth real manifold, use the symbol convention \(\sigma_m(A)(x,\xi)=\sum_{|\alpha|=m}a_\alpha(x)\xi^\alpha\), without factors of \(i\). Let \(m_f\) denote multiplication by \(f\), and define \(\delta_fA=[A,m_f]=A\circ m_f-m_f\circ A\). Then \(\delta_f^mA\) is multiplication by \(m!\,\sigma_m(A)(x,df_x)\). Thus the leading characteristic phase equation is \(\sigma_m(A)(x,df_x)=0\). Repeated commutators over a general ring need not permit division by \(m!\).',r'\citet[\S7]{vinogradov1972logic} publishes the symbol/Hamiltonian part of this programme in 1972. The principal-symbol and Hamilton--Jacobi concepts are older; Class A records the algebraic reformulation anticipated by this exercise, not a claim of priority for those concepts.',r'the scalar smooth identity is established and elementary. A Hamilton--Jacobi equation for a system or an inhomogeneous problem requires an additional specified principal-symbol equation.',**{'class':'A'},reconstruction=r'\ours The precise normalization follows by applying the product rule \(m\) times: all lower-order terms disappear and each top-order derivative contributes the corresponding symmetric product of \(df\), with \(m!\) permutations.',refs=V[:1]),
I('P000377-P000378','construction','The tensor-quotient construction of universal jets',r'The notes write \(J^s(M)=(K\otimes_{\mathbb Z}M)/\Pi_s\), where \(\Pi_s\) is generated by \((\bar k_0-k_0)\cdots(\bar k_s-k_s)\) applied to tensors.',r'For commutative \(K/R\), give \(K\otimes_R M\) the two actions \(a(b\otimes m)=ab\otimes m\) and \(\bar a(b\otimes m)=b\otimes am\). Let \(\Pi_s\) be the submodule generated by every \((\bar a_0-a_0)\cdots(\bar a_s-a_s)(b\otimes m)\). The quotient with \(j_s(m)=[1\otimes m]\) represents order-\(s\) operators: \(A\) corresponds to the unique homomorphism \([b\otimes m]\mapsto bA(m)\).',r'This is the older principal-parts universal construction, in \citet[\S16.8]{grothendieck1967egaiv4}; the lecture’s exact module form is restated in \citet[Proposition 7.1]{vinogradov2015logic}. The \(\mathbb Z\)-tensor in the notes correctly corresponds to additive, absolute operators.',r'the quotient construction works without smoothness, finite generation, or freeness. The base ring must remain fixed when one compares it with relative differential operators.',publication_match='exact',refs=G+V[1:]),
I('P000378-P000381','claim','Which universal forms recover ordinary smooth forms?',r'The representing modules \(\Lambda_i\) are identified with ordinary differential forms, while all \(K\)-modules are apparently allowed.',r'An identification with the ordinary smooth cotangent and exterior-form modules requires the geometric category over \(C^\infty(X)\), or an equivalent smooth-module universal property. Universal algebraic Kähler differentials representing derivations into every algebraic module can be larger. The distinction does not invalidate the differential-operator comparison for finite-rank bundle sections.',r'\citet[Proposition 7.2 and Example 7.1]{vinogradov2015logic} explicitly separates the two categories; its example warns that algebraic differentiation of \(e^x\) need not satisfy the ordinary smooth chain rule in the unrestricted universal module. This clarifies a suppressed hypothesis in the 1971 account.',r'the geometric-category reconstruction is established. Calling the unrestricted representing objects ordinary smooth forms is not literally correct.',**{'class':'A'},literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='partial',refs=V),
I('P000378-P000379','construction','Skew multiderivations and the distinction from covariant forms',r'The repeated derivation calculation yields \(D(a)(b)+D(b)(a)=0\). The notes then call \(D_k(M)\) ``\(k\)-forms with values in \(M\).'' In the two-coordinate calculation, several coefficients multiplying \(b\) are followed by \(\partial_x\) rather than \(\partial_y\).',r'The recursive objects are multiderivations. If 2 is invertible in the base, their skew symmetry gives alternating maps, and \(D_k(M)\cong\operatorname{Hom}_K(\bigwedge^k\Omega^1_{K/R},M)\). Over a smooth real manifold, with geometric modules, these are multivector fields with coefficients in \(M\); \(\Lambda_k\), the representing covariant object, is the form module. In characteristic two, skew symmetry alone does not impose alternation.',r'\citet[\S2]{vinogradov1972logic} publishes the recursive functors; \citet[\S5.2]{vinogradov2015logic} explicitly calls their smooth realizations multivector fields. The variance distinction repairs the notebook’s terminology.',r'the characteristic-zero interpretation is established. The characteristic-two examples must be examined with their exact functor and differential, rather than silently importing ordinary exterior powers.',**{'class':'A'},literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='exact',refs=V),
I('P000379','claim','The claimed nonzero second functor over the prime field',r'The bottom line reads \(K=\mathbb Z_2\), ``\(D_2\ne0\),'' followed by a parenthesis referring to second de Rham cohomology.',r'If \(\mathbb Z_2\) denotes the field \(\mathbb F_2\), the literal claim is false for the stated additive-derivation functors: every derivation from \(\mathbb F_2\) to a unital module is zero, so \(D_i(M)=0\) for every \(i\ge1\). A nonreduced algebra such as \(\mathbb F_2[C_2]\), considered on the next page, is a different object. Nonzero \(D_2\) is also not the same assertion as nonzero second cohomology.',r'No matching published statement was identified in the examined 1972 announcement or later survey. Their definitions suffice to reject the literal prime-field example; the neighbouring group-algebra example is a possible source of compression, not a justified silent substitution.',r'the literal prime-field assertion is refuted. The intended alternative example and the parenthetical identification remain unverified.',**{'class':'F'},literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',reconstruction=r'\ours A derivation satisfies \(D(1)=D(1\cdot1)=2D(1)\), hence \(D(1)=0\); additivity then makes it zero on the prime field. Induction in the notebook’s definition gives the higher vanishing.',refs=V,confidence='medium'),
I('P000380','claim','The group-ring/de Rham comparison is not identified',r'For the group algebra of the two-element group over \(\mathbb Z_2\), the notes claim all de Rham cohomology groups are \(\mathbb Z_2\), then assert that de Rham cohomology of group rings equals group cohomology. They contrast this with the diagonal-ideal definition of classical forms.',r'The claim requires a specified complex and coefficient convention. For ordinary exterior Kähler forms of \(A=\mathbb F_2[C_2]\cong\mathbb F_2[y]/(y^2)\), one obtains \(H^0_{\rm dR}(A/\mathbb F_2)=\mathbb F_2\), \(H^1_{\rm dR}(A/\mathbb F_2)=\mathbb F_2\), and zero in higher degrees. Thus this ordinary complex cannot realize the stated nonzero group in every degree. A different recursively defined characteristic-two complex cannot be identified solely from that numerical claim.',r'English and Russian searches for the group-ring comparison, and examination of \citet{vinogradov1972logic,vinogradov2015logic}, did not locate a theorem matching the unrestricted assertion. Its relation to later algebraic de Rham constructions remains unidentified.',r'no exact publication match for the notebook’s alternative theory is claimed. The ordinary de Rham interpretation is false; this does not establish that every conceivable corrected comparison fails.',**{'class':'F'},literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',reconstruction=r'\ours For the stated ordinary complex, \(\Omega^1=A\,dy\), \(\Omega^i=0\) for \(i\ge2\), and \(d(a+by)=b\,dy\). Its kernel and cokernel give the displayed calculation. This is an editorial diagnostic, not a computation of the unidentified alternative complex.',refs=V,confidence='low'),
I('P000379-P000381','theorem','Representation of multiderivations by universal forms',r'The lecture announces existence of \(\Lambda_i(K)\) with \(D_i(M)=\operatorname{Hom}_K(\Lambda_i(K),M)\).',r'In the commutative algebraic setting, the multiderivation functors are represented by universal modules. Over a base in which 2 is invertible, the standard alternating interpretation gives \(\Lambda_i(K)=\bigwedge^i\Omega^1_{K/R}\), with \(\Lambda_0=K\). Over general rings the exact skew-versus-alternating convention must be retained. Universal objects for a restricted smooth category are taken in that category.',r'The announcement \citet[\S5]{vinogradov1972logic} gives the representing-module formulation; \citet[\S7.2]{vinogradov2015logic} makes category dependence explicit. Kähler differentials and exterior powers themselves are older constructions.',r'the universal-property formulation is established. It does not identify multiderivations themselves with covariant forms, nor erase the characteristic-two distinction.',**{'class':'A'},publication_match='exact',refs=V),
I('P000380-P000382','construction','The exterior differential from a natural transformation',r'The inclusion \(D_i\to D_{i-1}(\operatorname{Diff}_1^+)\) is translated through universal objects into a first-order operator \(d:\Lambda_{i-1}\to\Lambda_i\). The notes check that consecutive compositions vanish.',r'In the commutative setting with a consistent form convention, the natural transformation is represented by a map \(J^1(\Lambda_{i-1})\to\Lambda_i\); composing with the universal jet gives \(d\). For ordinary alternating forms, \(d(a_0\,da_1\wedge\cdots\wedge da_i)=da_0\wedge da_1\wedge\cdots\wedge da_i\), which gives \(d^2=0\). The differential is first order over \(K\), generally not \(K\)-linear.',r'This natural-transformation construction is published in \citet[\S5]{vinogradov1972logic}; the representative/jet mechanism is restated in \citet[\S7.2]{vinogradov2015logic}. Class A concerns the categorical construction rather than the classical existence of \(d\).',r'the de Rham complex is established. Its cohomology depends on the ring, base, and module category; the disputed group-ring claim is not a consequence of representability alone.',**{'class':'A'},publication_match='exact',refs=V),
I('P000382-P000383','construction','Finite and stable algebraic Spencer complexes',r'Composition of operators and the functor inclusions give \(D_{i+1}(\operatorname{Diff}_k^+M)\to D_i(\operatorname{Diff}_{k+1}^+M)\). The finite and direct-limit chains are called a Spencer resolution, followed by the more cautious statement ``this is a complex.''',r'For each total order \(r\), the terms \(D_i(\operatorname{Diff}_{r-i}^+M)\) form a complex ending in \(\operatorname{Diff}_r^+M\to M\), with augmentation evaluation at 1. Taking the filtered union of finite-order operator modules gives the stable complex. For arbitrary commutative rings these complexes need not be exact; extra regularity and category assumptions are required to call them resolutions.',r'\citet[\S3]{vinogradov1972logic} explicitly states that its finite complex is exact in the last two terms, that the order-one complex is exact, and that higher complexes need not be exact. The smooth antecedent is \citet{spencer1969overdetermined}; the new feature is the functorial algebraic framework.',r'the complexes and their possible nonzero homology are established. The notebook’s universal word ``resolution'' must be qualified; its later word ``complex'' is the valid unrestricted assertion.',**{'class':'A'},literal_correctness='false as written',publication_match='exact',refs=V[:1]+['spencer1969overdetermined']),
I('P000383','problem','An index over an arbitrary ring is only a programme heading',r'The concluding headings include ``the index over any ring.'' No index group, admissible class of operators, or finiteness condition is given.',r'A mathematical index theorem here would need a defined class of operators, a target group for the index, hypotheses making the relevant kernel/cokernel or homological classes meaningful, and a comparison with a symbol construction. The notebook does not specify these data, so it does not pose one unique general theorem.',r'\citet[\S4 and concluding remarks of \S7]{vinogradov1972logic} relates Spencer homology to kernels and cokernels under acyclicity assumptions and announces a later algebraic index generalization. That is a concrete partial programme, but not a verified all-rings index theorem matching the bare heading.',r'the occurrence remains insufficiently specified for a separate problem-index entry or a claim of current openness. No blanket general index theorem is inferred from later uses of the same terminology.',**{'class':'F'},literal_correctness='not determinable',publication_match='thematic only',verification='read the result',refs=V[:1],confidence='medium'),
I('P000383','method','Hamiltonian formalism over a commutative algebra',r'The final list also promises a Hamiltonian formalism ``for every ring.''',r'For a commutative algebra \(K/R\), the order filtration on scalar differential operators has commutative associated graded algebra. The commutator induces a degree-minus-one bracket \(\{\sigma_p(A),\sigma_q(B)\}=\sigma_{p+q-1}([A,B])\). In the smooth real case this identifies with the usual Poisson bracket on fibre-polynomial functions on \(T^*X\), up to the chosen symbol sign convention. The corresponding statement for an arbitrary noncommutative ring is not supplied by these formulas.',r'\citet[\S7]{vinogradov1972logic} publishes precisely the symbol and Hamiltonian programme for commutative rings. This gives a close mathematical match to the heading once its ambient category is corrected.',r'the commutative symbol bracket is established. It does not provide one unrestricted Hamiltonian calculus on all noncommutative rings.',**{'class':'A'},literal_correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',publication_match='exact',reconstruction=r'The associated-graded bracket is the precise published reconstruction of an otherwise terse programme heading.',refs=V[:1])
])
